; =====================================================================
; dedenting-result.cddl
;
; VERBATIM.  RFC 9165's own CDDL, copied unchanged, margin intact.  NOT
; authored by this project.  This is the SOURCE's rendering of a computed
; result, not ours - the document states the equivalence itself: "For the
; first rule in Figure 3, the result is equivalent to Figure 4."
;
; Figure 4 (Section 2.3).  Redefines `oid`, hence the separate file.
;
; NOTE A NOTATIONAL DISCREPANCY, reported not repaired (README.md): Figure 4
; spells the result as a BYTE string literal, while Section 2.2's result rule
; - which Section 2.3 inherits by "works like .cat" - makes the result of
; `"oid" .det cbor-tags-oid` a TEXT string, because the target `"oid"` is a
; text string.
;
; Source digest (sha256 of the full cached source text):
;   94f86116c04fde0f11d54576465181090ec33983d78db82527c0703a41976774
; Source: RFC 9165, "Additional Control Operators for the Concise
;         Data Definition Language (CDDL)", C. Bormann, December
;         2021, Standards Track, ISSN 2070-1721.
;         <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9165.txt>
; =====================================================================

; Figure 4: Dedenting Example: Result of First .det
; ---- BEGIN VERBATIM F-4 (source lines 225-230, sha256 16228b1030aac9f2e4c607cf655ffc1b6f7ab0d2c7fbf3f7c6870f988b914b0d) ----
   oid = bytes .abnfb 'oid
   oid = 1*arc
   roid = *arc
   arc = [nlsb] %x00-7f
   nlsb = %x81-ff *%x80-ff
   '
; ---- END VERBATIM F-4 ----
